{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792170042,"nonce":"IG9j8PUq6SwKr+aJjL8YMiq2Ays=","title":"ScenePainter.render allocates several fresh arrays and a transform map every frame","message":"Found while investigating ChimpMonk editor memory growth during camera\norbit/pan (chimpmonk git-bug 789ec6b). ScenePainter.render rebuilt\ninView, entries, order, solid and groups as fresh arrays every call,\nand built a fresh ObjectMap plus a before-transforms array for motion\nblur's previous-frame lookup, every call, even though most scenes\n(including this editor's) have scene.motionBlur == 0 and never read\nthem. sceneBounds also allocated 2-element arrays and 2 Vec3s per\nbounding-box corner per draw, used only to fit shadow-cascade bounds,\ncomputed unconditionally regardless of whether the scene casts shadows.\n\nFix: inView/entries/order/solid/groups are now scratch fields resized\nin place rather than rebuilt (matching the existing drawBytes\npattern). The motion-blur transform history is only built when\nscene.motionBlur \u003e 0; otherwise before[i] is just the draw's own\ntransform, with no per-mesh ObjectMap/Array\u003cMat4\u003e bookkeeping.\nsceneBounds now accumulates the 8 corners' min/max in raw floats and\nconstructs the camera-matrix multiply inline, instead of allocating a\nVec3 and two 2-element arrays per corner.\n\nMeasured with a stress harness driving a 64-figure scene's camera\nsinusoidally (orbit-stress in chimpmonk): ScenePainter's own\nallocation (excluding the GameLayer.draw() it dispatches to, which is\nchimpmonk's own code) dropped from roughly 900KB-1MB/s to roughly\n200KB/s during continuous camera motion. Verified against the full\nrender pixel-diff suite (including the 3D selection/gizmo tests, which\nexercise this exact path) and the default test suite, both clean.","files":null}]}